.TH CLOG_SET_LEVEL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_set_level \- change the minimum log level at runtime
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_set_level(clog logger, clog_level_t level);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Updates the minimum severity level.  A later call that carries a level
strictly below the new minimum is silently discarded.  The change takes
effect at once.  Every thread that shares the logger sees it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
level   New minimum level (CLOG_TRACE through CLOG_OFF).
        Use CLOG_OFF to suppress all output except CLOG_FATAL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
CLOG_FATAL bypasses this filter entirely and is always written,
whatever the current minimum level is.
A log call on a different thread can run at the same time as this
call.  The minimum that applies to that log call can be the old one or
the new one.  On an async logger, the logger writes a record that is in
the queue when the change takes effect.
Thread-safe.  The minimum level is an atomic value that belongs to each
logger.  It is separate from the lock that serialises the writes and
the rotation for the shared output target.  A change to the level of
one logger therefore never contends with a write in progress on a
sibling logger that shares the same target.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_get_level (3),
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3)
